import AxQM.Postulates.API.PhaseFlipStabilizer

/-!
# Nielsen & Chuang, Exercise 10.46 — the phase-flip code stabilizer is generated by X₁X₂ and X₂X₃

*(N&C §10.5.6, p. 467.)*

Stabilizer for three qubit phase flip code is generated by X1X2 and X2X3.

## What the exercise asks

The three-qubit **phase-flip code** is the Hadamard conjugate of the bit-flip code: its logical
codewords are `|0_L⟩ = |+++⟩`, `|1_L⟩ = |−−−⟩` (`AxQM.phaseFlipCodeword`, used in the
QEC verification of Exercise 10.8). Where the bit-flip code has stabilizer `⟨Z₁Z₂, Z₂Z₃⟩` (N&C
p. 466), the phase-flip code has stabilizer `⟨X₁X₂, X₂X₃⟩` — the same generators with `X` (which
reads the `±` sign, `X|+⟩ = |+⟩`, `X|−⟩ = −|−⟩`) in place of `Z` (which reads the computational
sign). N&C states the bit-flip generators by inspection; this exercise asks to *show* the phase-flip
ones.

"The stabilizer is generated by `X₁X₂` and `X₂X₃`" has two halves, both formalized:

* **Containment** (`⟨X₁X₂, X₂X₃⟩ ⊆ S`) — `X₁X₂` and `X₂X₃` are commuting self-adjoint involutions,
  so the group they generate is the Klein-four group `⟨X₁X₂, X₂X₃⟩ = {I, X₁X₂, X₂X₃, X₁X₃}`, whose
  third element is the product `X₁X₃ = X₁X₂ · X₂X₃`
  (`AxQM.xxIObservable_op_mul_ixxObservable`).
  Every element of this group stabilizes the code: the identity trivially, and each of the three
  nontrivial elements fixes both codewords with eigenvalue `+1`. The three theorems below
  (`xxIObservable_hasEigenstate_one_phaseFlipCodewordState`,
  `ixxObservable_hasEigenstate_one_phaseFlipCodewordState`,
  `xixObservable_hasEigenstate_one_phaseFlipCodewordState`) prove this direction.
* **Maximality** (`S ⊆ ⟨X₁X₂, X₂X₃⟩`) — these two generate the *whole* stabilizer: the code space is
  *exactly* the joint `+1`-eigenspace of `X₁X₂` and `X₂X₃`, so the stabilizer has the full order
  `2^{3-1} = 4` and no further element can be adjoined. This is the operator identity
  `phaseFlipCodeProj = P₊(X₁X₂) ∘ P₊(X₂X₃)` (`AxQM.phaseFlipCodeProj_eq_projPlus_comp`),
  with the generators shown to commute (`xxIObservable_commute_ixxObservable`), both in
  `AxQM/Postulates/API/PhaseFlipStabilizerMaximality.lean`.

## The three theorems

The generators are the observables `xxIObservable = X ⊗ X ⊗ I` (`X₁X₂`) and
`ixxObservable = I ⊗ X ⊗ X` (`X₂X₃`) on the three-qubit register, and their product is
`xixObservable = X ⊗ I ⊗ X` (`X₁X₃`) (`AxQM/Postulates/API/PhaseFlipStabilizer.lean`). Each of
the three nontrivial stabilizer elements fixes both logical codewords with eigenvalue `+1`:

* `xxIObservable_hasEigenstate_one_phaseFlipCodewordState` — `X₁X₂ |σσσ⟩ = |σσσ⟩` for `σ ∈ {+, −}`.
* `ixxObservable_hasEigenstate_one_phaseFlipCodewordState` — `X₂X₃ |σσσ⟩ = |σσσ⟩`.
* `xixObservable_hasEigenstate_one_phaseFlipCodewordState` — `X₁X₃ |σσσ⟩ = |σσσ⟩` (the product
  element, so the whole group `⟨X₁X₂, X₂X₃⟩` stabilizes the code).

The mechanism is that `Xᵢ` reads the `±` sign of qubit `i` (`X|σ⟩ = (−1)^σ|σ⟩`), so `X₁X₂` acts on
`|σ₁σ₂σ₃⟩` by the sign `(−1)^{σ₁+σ₂}` (`xxIObservable_op_pmThreeVec`); on a codeword `|σσσ⟩` this
sign is `(−1)^{2σ} = +1`. This is the exact `±`-basis mirror of the bit-flip generators `Z₁Z₂`,
`Z₂Z₃` fixing the computational codewords `|000⟩`, `|111⟩`.

## Where the supporting results are

The `X₁X₂`, `X₂X₃`, `X₁X₃` observables, their `±`-basis action laws, the involution facts, and the
`PureState` codewords are in `AxQM/Postulates/API/PhaseFlipStabilizer.lean`; the group law
`X₁X₂ · X₂X₃ = X₁X₃`, the **maximality** identity `phaseFlipCodeProj = P₊(X₁X₂) ∘ P₊(X₂X₃)`, and the
generators' commutativity are in `AxQM/Postulates/API/PhaseFlipStabilizerMaximality.lean`. Compare
the analogous stabilizer-eigenstate statements of Exercise 10.5 (Shor phase syndrome) and
Exercise 10.32 (Steane Z-generators).
-/

open AxQM

namespace AxQM

/-- **Nielsen & Chuang, Exercise 10.46 (containment, generator `X₁X₂`).** The first phase-flip
stabilizer generator `X₁X₂ = X ⊗ X ⊗ I` fixes both logical codewords `|+++⟩`, `|−−−⟩`: each is a
`+1`-eigenstate. On `|σσσ⟩` the operator `X₁X₂` reads the sign `(−1)^{σ+σ} = +1`, so it stabilizes
the code. -/
theorem xxIObservable_hasEigenstate_one_phaseFlipCodewordState (a : Fin 2) :
    xxIObservable.HasEigenstate 1 (phaseFlipCodewordState a) := by
  refine Observable.hasEigenstate_of_smul (c := 1) (v := pmThreeVec (a, a, a))
    (by rw [phaseFlipCodewordState_vec, one_smul]) ?_
  rw [xxIObservable_op_pmThreeVec]
  fin_cases a <;> norm_num

/-- **Nielsen & Chuang, Exercise 10.46 (containment, generator `X₂X₃`).** The second phase-flip
stabilizer generator `X₂X₃ = I ⊗ X ⊗ X` fixes both logical codewords `|+++⟩`, `|−−−⟩`: each is a
`+1`-eigenstate. Together with `xxIObservable_hasEigenstate_one_phaseFlipCodewordState`, this shows
the group `⟨X₁X₂, X₂X₃⟩` stabilizes the phase-flip code. -/
theorem ixxObservable_hasEigenstate_one_phaseFlipCodewordState (a : Fin 2) :
    ixxObservable.HasEigenstate 1 (phaseFlipCodewordState a) := by
  refine Observable.hasEigenstate_of_smul (c := 1) (v := pmThreeVec (a, a, a))
    (by rw [phaseFlipCodewordState_vec, one_smul]) ?_
  rw [ixxObservable_op_pmThreeVec]
  fin_cases a <;> norm_num

/-- **Nielsen & Chuang, Exercise 10.46 (containment, product element `X₁X₃`).** The third
nontrivial stabilizer element `X₁X₃ = X ⊗ I ⊗ X`, the product `X₁X₂ · X₂X₃`
(`xxIObservable_op_mul_ixxObservable`), also fixes both logical codewords `|+++⟩`, `|−−−⟩`: each is
a `+1`-eigenstate. Together with the two generator results, this shows *every* element of the
group `⟨X₁X₂, X₂X₃⟩ = {I, X₁X₂, X₂X₃, X₁X₃}` stabilizes the phase-flip code (`⟨X₁X₂, X₂X₃⟩ ⊆ S`). -/
theorem xixObservable_hasEigenstate_one_phaseFlipCodewordState (a : Fin 2) :
    xixObservable.HasEigenstate 1 (phaseFlipCodewordState a) := by
  refine Observable.hasEigenstate_of_smul (c := 1) (v := pmThreeVec (a, a, a))
    (by rw [phaseFlipCodewordState_vec, one_smul]) ?_
  rw [xixObservable_op_pmThreeVec]
  fin_cases a <;> norm_num

end AxQM
